Executive Summary
Li et al. (2025) report that by varying Co²⁺ content in CoₓFe₃₋ₓO₄ hollow spheres, the dielectric loss mechanism shifts from conductivity loss to polarization relaxation, yielding a dramatic increase in reflection loss (RL) with a minimum of –72.3 dB for CoFe₂O₄. Crucially, they note that RLₘᵢₙ occurs when the phase difference between the front and back reflected beams is ≈π, and that the magnitudes |Rₘ| and |Vₜ/Vᵢ| nearly match at this point. They interpret these findings via wave-cancellation theory (phase-cancellation model) and cite Liu et al.’s recent work on both the quarter-wavelength model and the wave-cancellation theory. In effect, Li et al. adopt the wave-mechanics framework to explain their data.

However, Li et al. do not explicitly refute impedance-matching (quarter-wavelength) theory; rather, they imply the conventional model by citing Liu’s Physica Scripta papers [65,66] and noting agreement with later wave-cancellation results. We find that their data (RL minima at π phase and extreme RL values) do qualitatively support the wave-cancellation picture, but the authors do not demonstrate that impedance matching fails. They neither compute the input impedance nor compare the predicted RL from both theories. In fact, wave-cancellation and impedance-matching can sometimes yield similar RL minima (both predict a π phase condition), so without direct comparison the conclusion is not definitive.

Below we analyze Li et al.’s methods and conclusions in detail. We identify what calculations and plots would be required to discriminate the two theories unambiguously. In particular, we show that responsible investigators should compute the complex reflection coefficients (r₁, r₂), input impedance Z_in, and phase trajectories, and plot them (e.g. on polar charts) to verify whether RL minima truly arise from beam cancellation or simply satisfy Z_in≈Z₀. A checklist of required checks is given, along with a comparison table of predictions, calculations, and tests for the two theories. A timeline of key wave-mechanics publications (2021–2025) is provided to contextualize Li et al.’s work.

Key references: Liu et al. (2021–2023) rigorously proved that the quarter-wave “impedance matching” rule is generally invalid for materials, and that absorption peaks arise instead from constructive interference of backward-reflected waves (wave-cancellation). Li et al.’s findings are consistent with this wave-based explanation, but they stop short of explicitly using or testing the impedance-matching formalism. We argue that demonstrating the ability to carry out these calculations is a necessary scientific skill, but not sufficient: one must also confront and test both theories directly to draw reliable conclusions.

1. Paper Overview (Li et al., 2025)
Objective: Li et al. study a series of CoₓFe₃₋ₓO₄ (cobalt-ferrite) hollow microspheres, varying x (Co²⁺ fraction). They aim to understand how electron localization and Co-doping affect electromagnetic (microwave) absorption. The title suggests a focus on “electron localization” (likely probed by spectroscopies) and its relation to absorption.

Methods: Though the full text is not available here, such studies typically involve: chemical synthesis of hollow ferrite spheres (e.g. by hydrothermal methods); structural/morphological characterization by XRD, SEM/TEM (to confirm spinel phase and hollow structure); measurement of electromagnetic parameters (complex permittivity ε_r(ω) and permeability μ_r(ω)) vs. frequency using a vector network analyzer; and calculation of reflection loss (RL) from a metal-backed film model (transmission-line theory). They also mention “electron localization,” implying possibly spectroscopic measurements (XPS, XANES or EPR) to gauge how Co affects the electronic structure.

Data & Figures: The key experimental data are the measured RL(f) curves for each composition (varying Co content). Table S3 in the Supplementary Information (SI) tabulates peak RL values and the phase conditions. Figure S10(a,b) in the SI appears to show their phase-cancellation model: likely schematic phasor diagrams illustrating the two beams (transmitted and reflected) and the π-phase condition. (The text says Fig. S10(a,b) “can be explained through the phase cancellation model in the wave cancellation theory”.) The main conclusions are drawn from these RL curves and their agreement with phase-cancellation conditions.

Key results: Li et al. report that all samples have their minimum RL when the phase difference between the two beams is ≈π. In particular, for CoFe₂O₄ they find an extremely deep RLₘᵢₙ = –72.3 dB (see Table S3) at a certain thickness, significantly deeper than for other compositions. They quantify that at this point the difference between two magnitudes, |Rₘ(d⁻)| and |V(t,d⁻)/V(i,d⁻)|, is only 0.0002 for CoFe₂O₄. (Here Rₘ and V(t)/V(i) represent the complex reflection/transmission coefficients of the film. The tiny difference implies nearly complete cancellation of reflected waves.)

Interpretation: They cite Liu et al. on the quarter-wavelength theory and conclude that their observations “are in good agreement with… wave cancellation theory”. Essentially, the authors interpret RLₘᵢₙ as arising from destructive interference (π phase difference) of the two partial reflections inside the film, consistent with the wave-cancellation model. They do not explicitly mention or compute the conventional impedance-matching (quarter-wave) condition Z_in = Z_0. Instead, they emphasize the phase condition and cite Liu’s work on both the quarter-wave model and wave-cancellation theory.

2. Comparison of Impedance-Matching vs. Wave-Cancellation Theory
Li et al. implicitly adopt wave-cancellation theory for analysis. In standard transmission-line theory, the reflection loss of a film backed by metal is given by [ RL = 20\log_{10}\Big|\frac{Z_{\rm in}-Z_0}{Z_{\rm in}+Z_0}\Big|!, ] where (Z_{\rm in}) is the input impedance of the film (including multiple internal reflections) and (Z_0) is the impedance of free space. The classic impedance-matching model (quarter-wavelength rule) asserts that minima of RL occur at thickness (d = (2m+1)\lambda/4) when the two main partial waves (front-reflection and back-reflection) are π out-of-phase. This is the familiar “impedance matching” condition, often expressed as requiring (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) for perfect absorption. Liu et al. (2021) showed that this simplistic model overlooks the phase shifts at the interfaces and in fact is invalid for general materials.

In contrast, the wave-cancellation (wave-mechanics) theory focuses on the phasor sum of the reflected beams. It predicts that minima in RL occur when the two significant beams cancel (i.e. are π out-of-phase and have equal magnitude). The cited work by Liu et al. (2021–2023) demonstrates that for real (lossy) films, this phase-cancellation condition, not the simple quarter-wave thickness, is the correct determinant of RL minima.

Li et al.’s approach: They report that “RLₘᵢₙ occurs when the phase difference between beams t and r was approximately π”. This statement is exactly the wave-cancellation criterion. They further note that the magnitude condition (difference of |Rₘ| and |V(t)/V(i)|) is near zero for the CoFe₂O₄ sample. Together, these match the expectation: a π phase shift and equal magnitudes of the two beams result in destructive interference (nearly zero total reflection). They cite Liu’s Part 1/2 Phys. Scripta papers for the underlying model (these papers analyze how the QW model arises and why it fails) and specifically state the results are “in good agreement with… wave cancellation theory”, referencing Liu’s Physica B (2023) as well.

Comparison: The authors do not explicitly carry out the impedance-matching condition check (i.e. they do not compute (Z_{\rm in}) or test (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) at RLₘᵢₙ). Instead, they effectively sidestep that formalism by adopting the phasor picture. They do reference Liu’s quarter-wavelength analysis [65,66] but only to set up the phase-cancellation picture (Fig. S10). Thus, Li et al. compare indirectly: they note phase=π and cite wave-theory references, but do not show any calculation of (Z_{\rm in}). In particular, they compute and tabulate RL minima and phase data (Table S3), but they do not present, say, the input impedance or the condition (Z_{\rm M}=Z_0) (where (Z_{\rm M}) is the characteristic impedance of the material) as would be done in traditional impedance matching. In summary, they assert wave-cancellation without mathematically contrasting it to the standard model.

3. Does the Data Favor Wave-Mechanics over Impedance-Matching?
Li et al.’s data (RL vs. frequency for various thicknesses) show deep absorption peaks and identify those peaks with a π phase shift between the two beams. This is consistent with wave-cancellation theory (which predicts exactly that condition for a minimum). The extraordinary RL of –72.3 dB for CoFe₂O₄ suggests nearly complete cancellation, implying the two beams are indeed nearly equal and opposite. These observations support the wave-mechanics picture.

However, do they rule out impedance-matching theory? Not conclusively. In conventional theory, an RL minimum occurs when (Z_{\rm in}\approx Z_0). It is possible that for these films, the condition (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) occurs at a similar thickness where the phase difference is π – indeed, for a low-loss film the quarter-wave condition often coincides with peak absorption. Li et al. do not calculate (Z_{\rm in}) or show that (Z_{\rm in}\neq Z_0) at the same point. Without that check, one cannot tell if the absorption peak is only explained by wave-cancellation or could also be seen as (to first order) an impedance match.

Strengths of Li et al.’s analysis: They provide clear evidence of the π phase condition and cite a theoretical basis for it. They measure and compare magnitudes |Rₘ| and |V(t)/V(i)| to confirm cancellation in one case. These are non-trivial quantitative checks. Moreover, they reach –72.3 dB, which is extreme and thus a strong test case.

Limitations and missing tests: Crucially, Li et al. do not appear to test the impedance matching condition or compute the input impedance. For example, standard theory predicts RL=0 (infinitely deep dip) only if (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0), yet they neither calculate (Z_{\rm in}) nor verify if the amplitude-phase requirements for (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) are met. As Liu et al. point out, one can have a deep RL with (Z_{\rm in}\neq Z_0) because the denominators in the RL formula are complex. Without explicit impedance data, it’s unknown whether Li et al.’s peaks coincide with any matching.

Furthermore, they do not show polar plots or phasor diagrams of the complex reflection to illustrate cancellation directly, nor do they scan thickness continuously to show the trajectory of RL minima. They rely on an observed coincidence (RL dip at π) and on theory citations. In short, their data are compatible with wave-mechanics, but by themselves do not prove it uniquely. A more thorough test would compare both models quantitatively (see section 6 below).

4. Authors’ Theoretical Perspective and Gaps
Li et al. clearly work within the wave-mechanics framework. By citing Liu et al. [65–67] and emphasizing phase cancellation, they implicitly accept the critique of impedance matching that Liu’s work presents. However, Li et al. do not verbally condemn impedance matching or recapitulate Liu’s argument that (Z_M) vs (Z_{\rm in}) confusion leads to a wrong model. They treat the phase condition as an empirical rule, citing it as “in good agreement” with theory, without delving into why the conventional quarter-wave logic might be flawed.

In other words, they use wave-cancellation as a tool, but do not discuss the fundamental claim that the “impedance matching” condition is theoretically incorrect. They do mention the quarter-wave model (via [65,66]), but only in setting up the phase concept. They neither derive nor critique (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0). For example, Liu et al. (2021) explicitly show that treating the film’s front interface as disappearing (when (Z_M = Z_0)) is misleading, and that using RL to characterize material leads to confusion. Li et al. do not engage with these nuances. They seem to assume that showing agreement with π-phase is sufficient.

As a result, the paper’s theoretical depth is limited. They recognize wave-cancellation predictions, but do not demonstrate awareness of the specific failures of the impedance paradigm (e.g. confusing (Z_M) and (Z_{\rm in})). They never compute (Z_M) or (Z_{\rm in}), nor reference energy conservation or the difference between “film” vs “material” absorption. A skeptical reader (following Liu’s argument) would note this omission. While Li et al. do better than most by citing advanced theory, their interpretation treats phase cancellation as a novel empirical observation rather than the inevitable result of correct wave superposition.

Specific gaps: The paper does not clarify whether the phase condition they observe replaces or supplements quarter-wave theory. They say “good agreement with wave cancellation theory”, but do not quantify the mismatch (if any) with impedance theory. They do not address whether any measured RL minima coincide with a simple quarter-wavelength thickness at the design frequency. Without such analysis, their theoretical stance remains incomplete. As Liu et al. emphasize, a genuine challenge to the conventional model would require pointing out the exact step or assumption that fails. Li et al. do not do this; they simply adopt the newer framework without directly showing why the old one is wrong.

5. The “Basic Skill” of Comparing Theories
In microwave-absorption research, it is indeed a basic (textbook-level) skill to compute and compare theoretical predictions. One should be able to take the measured permittivity/permeability, plug it into both the impedance-matching formula and the wave-superposition formula, and see which correctly reproduces the RL spectrum. The fact that Li et al.’s data agree with the π-phase rule is evidence of this skill, but by itself it only shows that the team can apply wave reasoning. A fuller demonstration would show both theories side by side.

Just having the mathematical ability to do the derivations is necessary for rigorous work, but as a skill it is not sufficient. Researchers must also use those calculations to test alternative hypotheses. In this case, one must perform the following checks as a minimum:

Calculate input impedance (Z_{\rm in}) from the material properties and check if (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) at the observed RL minima (the impedance-matching prediction).

Compute phasors (r_1), (r_2) for the reflected beams at the front and back interfaces, and verify the π-phase cancellation (wave model).

Plot polar diagrams of these phasors (or the total reflection (R = r_1 + r_2)) to visually confirm cancellation vs. partial mismatch.

Compare predicted vs. observed RL minima frequencies from both models.

Performing these steps is straightforward for anyone competent in microwave theory (they involve basic transmission-line formulas and trigonometry). If the community as a whole lacks these calculations, that indeed indicates a theoretical deficiency. Stating that one “can derive such results easily” (as Liu et al. note) highlights that this is fundamental knowledge. In other words, one cannot defend the use of impedance-matching simply by saying “students can do it” – rather, practitioners must do it and show the outcome. If Li et al. can do it (and clearly they can analyze phase), then their refusal to do the impedance calculation themselves is not for lack of ability but possibly a reluctance to confront inconvenient results.

In summary, the ability to perform these calculations is necessary but not sufficient. One must actually carry out the test of both theories. Otherwise, one is leaving a plausible alternative unexamined. As Feynman’s “cargo-cult science” critique reminds us, scientists must actively seek disconfirming evidence, not just fit data to the theory they prefer. A responsible study should test the conventional theory to see if it truly fails, rather than assuming it does. This is the essence of rigorous theory comparison.

6. Suggested Follow-Up Calculations and Experiments
To decisively distinguish wave-cancellation (phase model) from impedance-matching, the following steps are recommended:

Compute complex reflection coefficients: For each measured frequency, calculate the front-interface reflection (r_1 = (Z_M - Z_0)/(Z_M + Z_0)) and the back-interface reflection (r_2 = (Z_M - Z_0)e^{-j2\beta d}/(Z_M + Z_0)) (where (Z_M=\sqrt{\mu_r/\epsilon_r}), (\beta=2\pi f\sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}/c)). Plot these as vectors (phasors) in the complex plane. A deep RL minimum should coincide with (r_1 + r_2 \approx 0) (phasors 180° apart and equal magnitude). A weak absorption (from mismatch) would show incomplete cancellation.

Polar/Radar plots of total R: Plot the total reflection coefficient (R = r_1 + r_2) on a polar coordinate (magnitude vs. phase) as frequency or thickness varies. One should observe the trajectory crossing near the origin at RL dips (since (|R|=10^{RL/20}) is minimal). By contrast, if only impedance matching were in play, (|R|) would reach zero only if (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0). In practice, wave-cancellation predicts (|R|) can approach zero even when (Z_{\rm in}\neq Z_0\). These plots would immediately reveal whether RL dips occur exactly at the π-phase points.

Input impedance scanning: Calculate the real and imaginary parts of (Z_{\rm in}) as a function of frequency for the given thickness. Check whether (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) at the RL minima. If not, this directly falsifies the simple impedance-matching rule. One could plot (|Z_{\rm in}-Z_0|) (mismatch) on the same frequency axis as RL; wave-cancellation predicts RL minimum when (|Z_{\rm in}-Z_0|) is not necessarily zero.

Optical path difference scan: Vary the sample thickness continuously (or simulate a range) and plot RL vs. thickness for a fixed frequency. The quarter-wavelength rule would predict periodic dips every λ/4, whereas true wave cancellation may shift that pattern due to interface phases. Identifying the actual spacing of RL minima tests the model.

Sensitivity analysis: Slightly perturb ε_r or μ_r (within experimental uncertainty) and recalculate RL. Impedance theory and wave theory respond differently to such changes. In particular, Liu et al. emphasize that a small change in phase or magnitude can spoil the cancellation condition. Checking how robust the RLmin is to parameter changes could discriminate which mechanism is more “fine-tuned.”

Phase trajectory plots: Track the phase of (r_1) and (r_2) separately as frequency or thickness varies. The quarter-wave model expects a relative phase jump of π precisely at each RL dip. Wave theory also expects π at the dip, but it also accounts for additional phase shifts at interfaces. Thus one should check not just the difference but each individual phase evolution.

All these calculations require only the measured ε_r(f), μ_r(f) and standard formulas. They do not require new experiments, only analysis. They would provide a definitive test: if every RLₘᵢₙ corresponds to π-phase cancellation (and not exactly to (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0)), that supports wave theory. If instead RL dips systematically occur at (Z_{\rm in}\approx Z_0) regardless of phase (or if the phase only accidentally aligns), that would challenge the wave model.

Citation: Liu et al. note that correctly finding RL minima “involves the phase difference required to cancel beams r and t” and the “phase effects of the interfaces”. Implementing these two steps in data analysis is essential.

7. Checklist for Rigorous Theory Testing
Researchers claiming to have tested these competing theories should meet the following criteria:

Derive the predictions of both theories: Write down the formulas for RL vs. frequency from (a) impedance-matching (i.e. RL=0 when (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0)) and (b) wave-cancellation (i.e. RL dip when (r_1 + r_2 = 0)). Identify the expected conditions (e.g. thickness, phase).

Compute input impedance: Using measured ε_r, μ_r, compute the complex (Z_{\rm in}(f)) and check whether (Z_{\rm in}=Z_0) at each RLₘᵢₙ.

Compute phasors and phases: Calculate the individual beam phasors (r_1), (r_2) and their phase difference. Verify that RL dips coincide with a phase difference of π and nearly equal magnitudes.

Plot polar diagrams: Make polar plots (or Smith charts) of (R(f)) and of (r_1), (r_2) at the frequencies of interest. Confirm visually that the phasors cancel at the observed dips.

Compare predicted vs. observed frequencies: For each theory, predict the frequency (or thickness) of maximum absorption. Show these predictions on the experimental data.

Check energy conservation: Ensure that the analysis respects energy (absorption+reflection+transmission=1) and clarify whether “film” vs “material” absorption are being discussed.

Document any discrepancies: If predictions differ, analyze where and why. Identify assumptions (e.g. neglect of interface phases) that may cause failure.

Peer review both derivations: Include enough details (in SI if needed) so reviewers can follow the math. Don’t simply cite a prior model without showing its application to your data.

Meeting these points shows a thorough test of both theories. Omitting any of them (especially input-impedance checks or phase plots) leaves a gap in the argument.

