10. Conclusions
Li et al. (2025) present valuable experimental results on CoFe₃O₄-based absorbers and interpret them via phase-cancellation. Their data (RL minima at π phase, extreme RL values) support the modern wave-interference perspective. However, to claim the impedance-matching model is wrong, one must explicitly demonstrate its failure. As shown by Liu et al., this requires examining the input impedance and interface phases in detail. We recommend that future work in this field always performs these basic calculations to truly compare theories.

In summary, detecting the π-phase condition is a significant consistency check, but it is only half the story. The scientific community should insist on full, quantitative comparisons – in other words, on doing the simple math – before dismissing either model. Only then can we avoid the “cargo-cult” of untested assumptions and confidently advance the understanding of microwave absorption.

